WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:11.689
Two services debit Ivy's account 109 at the same moment: a card payment of 1,000 and a monthly fee of 1,000.

2
00:00:11.689 --> 00:00:15.684
The balance is 5,000, so it should end at 3,000.

3
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:21.083
Each one reads the balance, subtracts in Kotlin, and writes the result.

4
00:00:21.083 --> 00:00:22.259
Both read 5,000.

5
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.606
The payment writes 4,000 and commits.

6
00:00:25.606 --> 00:00:28.283
Then the fee writes 4,000 and commits.

7
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.036
Two debits ran, and the balance fell by only 1,000.

8
00:00:32.036 --> 00:00:35.918
That's a lost update: the second write erased the first.

9
00:00:35.918 --> 00:00:38.760
Nothing failed, so no log line tells you.

10
00:00:38.967 --> 00:00:41.961
The database did put the two writes in order.

11
00:00:41.961 --> 00:00:46.020
But the fee's 4,000 came from a read that was no longer true.

12
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.071
The fix: lock the row when you read it, with SELECT FOR UPDATE.

13
00:00:51.071 --> 00:00:52.850
Start again from 5,000.

14
00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:56.097
The payment reads 5,000 and locks row 109.

15
00:00:56.267 --> 00:01:00.412
The fee asks for the same row, and its read now waits for that lock.

16
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.900
The payment writes 4,000 and commits, which releases the lock.

17
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.784
Only then does the fee's read return, and it sees 4,000.

18
00:01:08.967 --> 00:01:10.499
It writes 3,000.

19
00:01:10.499 --> 00:01:12.223
Both debits count.

20
00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:14.423
A job queue has the same bug.

21
00:01:14.423 --> 00:01:19.844
Five jobs are due, and three workers each claim the oldest one with FOR UPDATE.

22
00:01:19.844 --> 00:01:21.285
All three pick job 2.

23
00:01:21.467 --> 00:01:22.695
Worker 1 locks it.

24
00:01:22.695 --> 00:01:29.245
The other two wait for the whole job, about 2 seconds, so three workers run at the speed of one.

25
00:01:29.433 --> 00:01:31.594
But a worker doesn't need job 2.

26
00:01:31.594 --> 00:01:32.944
Any due job will do.

27
00:01:32.944 --> 00:01:37.197
With SKIP LOCKED, a locked row is skipped instead of waited on.

28
00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:40.413
Worker 2 skips job 2 and locks job 3.

29
00:01:40.413 --> 00:01:43.377
Worker 3 skips both and locks job 5.

30
00:01:43.377 --> 00:01:46.752
Nobody waits, and three jobs run at once.

31
00:01:46.933 --> 00:01:50.032
Reading, computing and writing back loses updates.

32
00:01:50.032 --> 00:01:54.371
Lock the row you read, and when any row will do, skip the locked ones.
